@meta
id: 25-04
title: The address that searches
chapter: 25
type: lesson
skills: range-pattern
par: 19
minutes: 5
vscode: Ctrl-F the word, click the line, Home, shift-Down, Delete
open: order.repository.ts

@brief
A `console.log` survived review and is sitting in `findMany`. Delete that
line. There is exactly one.

Every address so far has been a number in disguise — a line you counted
to, or `.` and `$` with arithmetic. The last kind of address is a
pattern: `/console/` means "the next line below the cursor that matches
`console`". It is the same search you type after `/`, used as a line
number.

So you do not have to go there first. The address goes and the command
follows.

@keys
| `:/console/d` | delete the next line matching `console` |
| `:/pat/` | as an address: the next line below the cursor matching `pat` |
| `:/pat/y` | yank it, `:/pat/m0` move it, and so on |

@hints
The address is a search between two slashes, used exactly where a line
number would go.
--
The pattern only has to be enough to pick the line out. `console` is
plenty.
--
`:/console/d` Enter, then `:wq`. The cursor never leaves line 1.

@solution
    :/console/d<Enter>  :wq<Enter>

Sixteen keystrokes, and note what you did *not* do: you did not search,
look at where you landed, and then delete. You described the line and
deleted it in one sentence.

Three things worth knowing about pattern addresses:

They search **forward from the line below the cursor**, and they wrap
around the end of the file, so from line 1 you reach anything. The line
the cursor is on is not considered — `:/console/d` run twice deletes two
different lines.

They take the **last-used pattern's options**, which means `ignorecase`
applies. `/console/` would match `Console` too. Chapter 13's `\C` forces
case sensitivity when that matters.

They are **the same regex engine** as `/` and `:s`, so `\<` `\>`, `\v`
and groups all work: `:/\<order\>/d` is an address as much as a search.

The trailing slash is optional when nothing follows the pattern — `:/console d`
would be read as part of the pattern, so keep the slash and you will
never have to think about it.

@start order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord, PrismaClient } from '@prisma/client';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository {
  constructor(protected prisma: PrismaClient) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const record = await this.prisma.order.findUnique({ where: { id } });

    return record ? this.toEntity(record) : undefined;
  }

  public async findMany(customerId: string) {
    const records = await this.prisma.order.findMany({ where: { customerId } });
    console.log('findMany returned', records.length);

    return records.map((record) => this.toEntity(record));
  }

  public toEntity(record: OrderRecord) {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      customerId: record.customerId,
      status: record.status,
      cancellationReason: record.cancellationReason ?? undefined,
    };
  }
}

@expected order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord, PrismaClient } from '@prisma/client';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository {
  constructor(protected prisma: PrismaClient) {}

  public async findOne(id: string) {
    const record = await this.prisma.order.findUnique({ where: { id } });

    return record ? this.toEntity(record) : undefined;
  }

  public async findMany(customerId: string) {
    const records = await this.prisma.order.findMany({ where: { customerId } });

    return records.map((record) => this.toEntity(record));
  }

  public toEntity(record: OrderRecord) {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      customerId: record.customerId,
      status: record.status,
      cancellationReason: record.cancellationReason ?? undefined,
    };
  }
}
